WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.800
1989 was a great year for pop culture classics,
with blockbuster hits that helped to form

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.099
some of the fabric of our shared language
of references and influences.

00:00:09.099 --> 00:00:13.519
It was also a full 30 years ago, and remembering
that while you’re watching an old favorite

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.740
might just make you feel like you’re crumbling
to dust as the credits roll.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:17.720
The good news?

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.500
We’re here with some emotional support as
you remember that these movies were released

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.440
a full three decades ago.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:29.460
In the 1980s, the only big-screen superhero
megastar was Superman, and the last major

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.940
live-action effort for Batman starred Adam
West.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:36.180
Then came director Tim Burton, fresh off of
Beetlejuice, and an effort to capture some

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.670
of the more gothic Batman spirit made so popular
by the comics of the '70s and '80s.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:45.420
The result was Tim Burton’s Batman, a massive
commercial hit and one of the most talked-about

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.020
films of 1989.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.150
Michael Keaton starred as the Dark Knight,
which was a controversial casting choice at

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.750
the time, since he was mostly known for known
for goofy comedies.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:57.900
It’s easy to argue that the real star was
Jack Nicholson as the Joker.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:00.580
"Wait’ll they get a load of me."

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.600
Burton and designer Anton Furst imagined a
Gotham City that lives up to its name with

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.729
bold set design choices, memorable costumes,
a gorgeously lonesome Wayne Manor, and a sweeping

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:14.380
score from Danny Elfman that still resonates
as Batman’s signature theme.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:19.240
The film has its flaws, including a rather
contrived origin for the Joker, but even today

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.320
it holds up as a classic of the genre.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:26.660
Other Batman films have come since, some great
and some… not so well loved, but Burton's

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:30.400
film still holds a certain kind of sway over
'80s and '90s kids.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.290
It spawned a franchise that would run for
a decade and remains one of the most influential

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:35.900
superhero films ever made.

00:01:36.049 --> 00:01:41.200
In fact, virtually every comic book film of
the 1990s and even into the 2000s was a reaction

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.200
to it in some way.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:47.259
The last film installment of the Friday the
13th franchise was an ill-fated reboot in

00:01:47.259 --> 00:01:52.009
2009, so it might be difficult to remember
that this slasher series was once a box office

00:01:52.009 --> 00:01:52.860
powerhouse.

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:58.160
Producers cranked out eight Friday films in
the 1980s, and quickly established Jason Voorhees'

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.720
reputation as an iconic killing machine who
just won't go away.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:06.220
Jason Takes Manhattan is not the best of these
films, but it is among the most interesting.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.840
It follows a group of high school seniors
who are all set to take a big trip up to the

00:02:09.849 --> 00:02:14.310
Big Apple, but there's one problem: their
journey begins at Crystal Lake, where Jason

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:15.740
sank at the end of the last film.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:20.660
He's reanimated by an electrical accident,
climbs aboard, and begins causing havoc.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.840
Sadly, he only really "takes" Manhattan in
the final minutes, but in between there's

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.220
a brutal and often silly cruise that some
Friday fans love and others loathe.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.620
Whether you dig the film or not, it remains
a fascinating cultural artifact in terms of

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:38.100
how far slasher franchises were willing to
go in the '80s to keep churning out sequels.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:43.600
Rick Moranis hasn't made a major live-action
film appearance in more than 20 years, but

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:47.800
once upon a time he was a comedy powerhouse,
and Honey, I Shrunk the Kids was one of his

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.500
most successful showcases.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:54.820
Moranis is a master of in-over-your-head,
nerdy comedy, and in that regard he was perhaps

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:57.300
never more perfectly cast than he is in this
film.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.440
"Dianne, I've got something real important
to tell you."

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.160
"Are you trying to tell me the machine works?

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:03.440
Do the kids know?"

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.280
"Well, yeah, the kids know."

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:06.520
"That's great!"

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:12.210
Suburban dad and amateur inventor Wayne Szalinski
has created a shrink ray that never seems

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:16.430
to quite work, until the day it accidentally
shrinks the Szalinski kids and their neighbors,

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:17.570
the Thompson children.

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:21.250
While Wayne and his wife Diane try desperately
to find the kids while keeping the incident

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:25.060
under wraps, the kids themselves embark on
an epic journey through the back yard.

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:29.080
Kids of a certain age watched this film over
and over when it hit home video.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.040
The middle entry into the Back to the Future
seems half-forgotten by some viewers, stuck

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:38.160
between "the original" and "the one where
they go to the Old West," but in many ways,

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:41.160
Back to the Future Part II is the most interesting
of the three films.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:45.220
For one thing, it's the only one of the three
that imagines a version of "the future”

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:50.160
of 2015 rather than jumping to the past, and
for another, that future is just bonkers.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.480
"Hydrate level four please."

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:00.799
Though it's definitely weaker than its predecessor,
Part II is full of instantly memorable moments,

00:04:00.799 --> 00:04:05.330
from the introduction of the hoverboard to
Biff's lair in the bad timeline to the ridiculous

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:07.480
fashion choices of "2015."

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:11.720
Plus, we will never stop making jokes about
the power of sports almanacs because of this

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:12.220
movie.

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:17.400
With one line, Field of Dreams cemented its
place in the pop culture lexicon as one of

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.340
the most quoted films ever made.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:24.100
"If you build it, he will come."

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:32.180
Really, though this movie is so much more
than a memorable phrase.

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:36.190
It’s a sweeping declaration of American
optimism, and the kind of movie that will

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:40.111
make even the most die-hard baseball haters
in your life fall in love with the game, if

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:41.040
only for a little while.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:45.460
Field of Dreams didn't need the benefit of
time for people to recognize its greatness.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:49.860
It was nominated for three Oscars after its
release, including Best Picture, and immediately

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:54.360
became the kind of film certain families wanted
to rent on home video again and again.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:58.520
In the years since, it's become the kind of
basic cable staple on which pretty much everyone

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.990
in the living room can agree, and even if
you're seeing it for the hundredth time, there's

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:03.980
a certain kind of magic in its simplicity.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:06.860
"Is this heaven?"

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:12.320
"It’s Iowa."

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:18.800
Even the worst Star Trek films are still interesting
in their ambition, and Star Trek V: The Final

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.880
Frontier is perhaps the best example of this.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:25.800
Directed by William Shatner, the film chronicles
the Enterprise crew's involvement in a quest

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.720
to find "God" in a mystical location somewhere
in the center of the galaxy.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:33.840
It's a big idea, and even though the results
have earned it a reputation among some Trek

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:37.760
fans as the single worst piece of the entire
franchise, it has its moments.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:40.980
"Excuse me, I’d just like to ask a question?

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:44.860
What does God need with a Starship?"

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:50.720
Of course, that power is masked by questionable
directing choices, subpar visual effects,

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:53.520
and a high concept that just kind of fizzles
out by the end.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:58.180
Despite all that, it still maintains a sense
of personality that makes it pretty watchable.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:01.410
Captain Kirk climbing a mountain and hanging
out with Spock and Bones around a campfire

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:02.640
together just works.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:07.740
Stephen King adaptations are culturally ubiquitous,
and they have been for decades.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:12.140
The man's work is so popular among film producers
that he's launched whole franchises from a

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:16.770
single short story, and filmmakers are often
so eager to capitalize on his name that they'll

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:19.540
only very loosely tie their stories to his
concepts.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:21.580
We’re looking at you here, Lawnmower Man.

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:33.820
When an adaptation works, though, it really
works, and Pet Sematary is definitely one

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:34.830
of those.

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:39.300
The film famously tells the story of a family
who moves into a new house near a pet cemetery

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:43.560
set up by the local children to bury the animals
who are hit by cars on the dangerous nearby

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:44.060
road.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.560
When the family cat dies, he's buried it the
Native American burial ground beyond the pet

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:52.740
cemetery, only to be brought back to life,
in a manner of speaking, by the land's mysterious

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:53.360
powers.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:58.099
Then the family's young son is killed… and
the rest is the stuff of nightmares.

00:06:58.099 --> 00:07:02.580
The climactic scares of Pet Sematary are still
among the most memorable of any horror film

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:06.770
released in the 1980s, and the story is so
powerful that it's easy to see why a remake

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:08.180
is hitting theaters this spring.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:13.680
For a very long time, Indiana Jones and the
Last Crusade was the final entry in the whip-cracking

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:17.810
adventure franchise and served as the perfect
way for Indy to quite literally ride into

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:18.360
the sunset.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:23.120
After the misstep of The Temple of Doom, filmmakers
George Lucas and Steven Spielberg returned

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:27.280
to the trope of Indy searching for fabled
religious artifacts and punching out Nazis,

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:30.800
and sent him after the “Holy Grail” of
ancient artifacts, which in this case was

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:32.360
the literal Holy Grail.

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:35.820
"Germany has declared war on the Jones boys!"

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:40.300
The result is a film that's not quite as good
as the first, because Raiders of the Lost

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.980
Ark is basically perfect, and something that
still holds up three decades later.

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:48.860
The chemistry between Indy and his father,
Henry Jones Sr, played by the great Sean Connery,

00:07:48.879 --> 00:07:52.979
is perfect, and while It's not quite the action
masterpiece that Raiders was, it's still great

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:53.780
adventure fun.

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:58.139
Plus, it made us say “he chose… poorly”
forever, even if we were just talking about

00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:59.140
pizza toppings.

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:03.500
The canon of great teen comedies of the 1980s
is vast and varied.

00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:07.410
You have your unrepentant sex comedies like
Porky's, your slightly more contemplative

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:11.102
fare like Sixteen Candles, your dramedies
like The Breakfast Club, and plenty of other

00:08:11.102 --> 00:08:14.260
movies that easily fit into a genre, or even
bounce between a few of them.

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:17.600
Then you've got Heathers, which is something
all its own.

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:22.580
"I've cut off Heather Chandler's head and
Heather Duke's head has sprouted back in its

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:23.080
place."

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:27.020
Heathers took the sense of aggression and
venom that was bubbling under the surface

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:31.580
of so many other teen comedies and put it
front and center, creating a deliciously nasty

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:34.160
brew that's as brutal as it is brutally hilarious.

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:38.700
Winona Ryder gives one of the best performances
of her career, and the film cemented Christian

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:41.469
Slater and Shannen Doherty as icons of their
era.

00:08:41.469 --> 00:08:45.250
Though its box office left something to be
desired, it became one of the most important

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:47.320
cult films of the early '90s video market.

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:51.370
We're still feeling its influence today in
everything from horror movies to YA novels

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:52.740
to its own TV remake.

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:57.240
Speaking of teen comedies from the '80s that
are in a class by themselves, there's Bill

00:08:57.250 --> 00:09:02.020
and Ted's Excellent Adventure, a film that
combines slacker culture with time travel.

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:05.180
If you haven't seen it, the results are exactly
as weird as you'd expect.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:07.300
"Ted?"

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:08.920
"What?"

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:12.560
"This has been a most unusual day."

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:17.120
The great thing about Bill and Ted, and what's
allowed it to hold up as well as it has for

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:20.830
30 years, is the very real sense of sincerity
running throughout the film.

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:25.010
It delivers the time travel goods and the
slacker comedy goods simultaneously, and it

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:29.260
somehow manages to combine all of that with
a kind of wish fulfillment as the boys gather

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:33.800
a cadre of historical figures together and
cram them into a time-traveling phone booth.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:37.600
Most importantly, though, there's a tremendous
self-awareness running through the film that

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:41.540
allows Bill and Ted to be both genuinely likable
and viciously self-deprecating at the same

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:42.040
time.

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:45.840
It's a deceptively simple movie with some
very complex comedy running through it.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:48.800
Oh, and it makes you want to bust out your
best air guitar.

